    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Patterns of structure contours on normal-faulted horizons depend upon the ratio of fault length in the dip and strike directions (fault aspect ratio) and the distribution of slip over the fault. To investigate these relationships, faults are modeled with elastic dislocations using the computer program Poly3D. For the range of reported fault aspect ratios, structure contour maps may be very useful for constraining fault geometry. This is demonstrated using a new methodology for deducing fault height from 3D seismic data that image faults on a selected horizon(s). The methodology is applied to a set of faults in the Prudhoe Bay oil field on the North Slope of Alaska that are well resolved on shallow horizons, but poorly imaged with increasing depth. The displacement fields due to the imposed slip boundary conditions on the interpreted faults are subtracted from the well-constrained horizon to produce a restored “pre-faulting” horizon (Figure 1). This procedure is repeated for different postulated fault geometries and estimates of fault height are drawn from those models that do the best job of restoring the horizon to the homoclinal structure inferred from the tectonic history of this region. The results predict that the faults offset the bottom of the reservoir, and that significant quantities of oil may remain in structural depressions in the hangingwalls where the reservoir has been displaced below the gas-oil contact.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
